<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">3 May in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">9 events that happened on 3 May, from 752 to 1937.</p></header>
<div class="tih-pinned-card"><p class="tih-pinned-label">International day</p><p class="tih-pinned-title">World Press Freedom Day</p><p class="tih-pinned-desc">Proclaimed by the UN General Assembly in 1993 on the anniversary of the Windhoek Declaration, World Press Freedom Day affirms that a free press is essential to democracy and evaluates the state of press freedom worldwide.</p></div>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/yaxuun-bahlam-iv" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yaxuun-bahlam-iv.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">752 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,274 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Bird Jaguar IV becomes king of Yaxchilan</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaxuun-bahlam-iv">Bird Jaguar IV</a> became king of Yaxchilan, a Maya city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usumacinta-river">Usumacinta River</a> in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiapas">Chiapas</a>, and ruled until 768. Not perceived as the rightful heir, he had to struggle to take and hold power.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiapas">Chiapas</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">996 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,030 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Otto III's cousin Bruno of Carinthia becomes Pope Gregory V</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Following the death of Pope John XV, the German king Otto III chose his cousin Bruno of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, a member of the Salian dynasty, as pope. Bruno took the name Gregory V, and Otto became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> the same year.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Otto III</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1469">1469 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">557 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Niccolò Machiavelli is born in Florence</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Machiavelli</a>, diplomat of the Florentine republic and author of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-prince">The Prince</a>, was born. His unsentimental analysis of power made him a founder of modern political thought.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1494">1494 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">532 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Columbus sights Jamaica on his second voyage</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> called Xaymaca, and named it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>. The island remained under Spanish rule until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> captured it in 1655.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1660">1660 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">366 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Treaty of Oliva makes peace between Sweden and Poland</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Signed near Danzig, the treaty ended the war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s king renounced his claim to the Swedish throne and recognised Swedish rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1791">1791 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">235 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Poland-Lithuania adopts the Constitution of 3 May</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> passed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s first modern codified national constitution, strengthening the monarchy and ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberum-veto">liberum veto</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> responded with war and the second partition.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1814">1814 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">212 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Napoleon arrives off Elba to begin his exile</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">HMS Undaunted brought the deposed emperor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portoferraio">Portoferraio</a>, where he landed the next morning to rule his tiny island principality. He escaped in February 1815.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1921">1921 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">105 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Northern Ireland comes into being</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Government of Ireland Act took effect, partitioning Ireland and creating Northern Ireland from six north-eastern counties with its own parliament. Partition remains in place today.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1937">1937 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">89 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Margaret Mitchell wins the Pulitzer for Gone with the Wind</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Margaret Mitchell's novel of the Civil War and Reconstruction won the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction. A huge bestseller, it was filmed in 1939, though its portrayal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> is now widely criticised.</p></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 3 May <span class="n">4</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/margaret-of-york"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/margaret-of-york.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Margaret of York</span><span class="tp">1446–1503</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/niccolo-machiavelli.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Niccolò Machiavelli</span><span class="tp">1469–1527</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/august-von-kotzebue"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/august-von-kotzebue.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">August von Kotzebue</span><span class="tp">1761–1819</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/pavlo-skoropadsky"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pavlo-skoropadsky.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Pavlo Skoropadsky</span><span class="tp">1873–1945</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 3 May <span class="n">5</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/matilda-i-countess-of-boulogne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/matilda-i-countess-of-boulogne.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Matilda I, Countess of Boulogne</span><span class="tp">c. 1105–1152</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/bela-iv-of-hungary"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bela-iv-of-hungary.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Béla IV of Hungary</span><span class="tp">1206–1270</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/john-i-duke-of-brabant"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-i-duke-of-brabant.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">John I, Duke of Brabant</span><span class="tp">c. 1252–1294</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/alexios-ii-of-trebizond"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/alexios-ii-of-trebizond.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Alexios II of Trebizond</span><span class="tp">1282–1330</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/benedict-xiv"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/benedict-xiv.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Benedict XIV</span><span class="tp">1675–1758</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
